WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.423
Batch tuning without measurement quickly becomes guesswork.

00:00:02.423 --> 00:00:05.741
Start with a repeatable benchmark that reflects the real workload.

00:00:05.741 --> 00:00:09.391
Use consistent prompts, fixed context lengths, the same model and quantization,

00:00:09.391 --> 00:00:10.718
and a quiet machine.

00:00:10.718 --> 00:00:15.032
Record the runtime version and hardware state so you can explain results later.

00:00:15.032 --> 00:00:17.022
A quick test can be useful,

00:00:17.022 --> 00:00:20.672
but a repeatable test is what lets you compare settings honestly.

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:22.331
Measure more than one number.

00:00:22.331 --> 00:00:26.313
Prompt processing speed tells you how quickly the runtime handles existing context.

00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:28.968
Generation speed, often reported as tokens per second,

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:31.290
tells you how quickly new tokens appear.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.277
First-token latency tells you how responsive the system feels.

00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:37.927
Total response time shows what the user experiences end to end.

00:00:37.927 --> 00:00:41.245
For services, also track requests per second and queue time.

00:00:41.245 --> 00:00:43.567
Watch resource behavior while the benchmark runs.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:46.554
GPU utilization, CPU utilization, VRAM use, system RAM, temperatures,

00:00:46.554 --> 00:00:49.872
and throttling can explain why a setting improved or failed.

00:00:49.872 --> 00:00:53.854
A result that looks fast during a cool one-minute test may degrade

00:00:53.854 --> 00:00:55.513
after the hardware heats up.

00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:59.494
Background tasks, driver activity, caching, and warmup effects can also distort results.

00:00:59.494 --> 00:01:02.149
Change one setting at a time whenever possible.

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:04.803
If you increase batch size, change context length,

00:01:04.803 --> 00:01:07.126
and raise concurrency in the same test,

00:01:07.126 --> 00:01:11.440
you may get a number but you will not know what caused it.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.426
Keep a simple table of settings, metrics, and observations.

00:01:14.426 --> 00:01:19.071
The discipline of measurement is what turns tuning from folklore into an engineering process.

00:01:19.071 --> 00:01:20.067
For team use,

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:24.380
write down the benchmark as if someone else will repeat it next month.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.362
Include the exact prompt shape, generation limit, runtime version, model file, quantization,

00:01:28.362 --> 00:01:31.017
hardware, driver version, and the settings being compared.

00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:33.671
The notes do not need to be elaborate,

00:01:33.671 --> 00:01:38.980
but they should be specific enough to prevent a future tuning conversation from becoming guesswork again.
